<Project>
  <!--
    The publisher, as the LICENSE and TRADEMARK_POLICY state it: the brand is "the fear", thefear078 is
    the account behind it. These land in the .exe's own version resource, so Windows' file properties,
    the installer's publisher field and the in-app About screen all name the same author from one
    source. A build that keeps the name while changing the money path is the thing users are asked to
    watch for; the name has to be somewhere they can check it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Company>the fear</Company>
    <Authors>the fear (thefear078)</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2026 the fear (thefear078). All rights reserved.</Copyright>
    <Product>Umbrella Wallet</Product>
    <Description>Umbrella Wallet — non-custodial desktop crypto wallet by the fear. Source-available under the Free-Use, No-Derivatives licence.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deterministic output: the same source must compile to the same bytes, on any machine, from any
    directory.

    This is the prerequisite for reproducible builds, and reproducible builds are what make "verify
    the SHA256" mean anything. Without them a user checking a checksum is comparing the download
    against a manifest published by the same release an attacker would have had to compromise. With
    them, anyone can rebuild from the tag and prove the binary is the source.

    `Deterministic` is already the .NET default and it works: two builds in the same directory were
    byte-identical before this file said anything. What was NOT identical was the same source built
    from a DIFFERENT directory - the compiler embeds absolute source paths, so `D:\WorkSpace\...` and
    `/home/runner/work/...` produce different assemblies. That is precisely the case that matters,
    because the person verifying is never building in the same folder as the person who released.

    PathMap rewrites those embedded paths to a fixed token, which removes the difference without
    pulling in SourceLink. Measured both ways: see docs/building.md.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <PathMap>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)=/_/</PathMap>

    <!--
      Symbols are the one thing that stops a Release build being reproducible, and it took measuring
      to find out why: the debug directory embeds the ABSOLUTE PATH of the .pdb, and the MVID depends
      on it. Two builds of the same commit from different folders differed by 735 bytes, and the block
      that differed was exactly 29 bytes longer on one side - the difference in the length of that
      path.

      Measured, all three, same commit, two different absolute paths:
        portable  -> differs (735 bytes)
        embedded  -> differs (the embedded blob is path-dependent too)
        none      -> BYTE-IDENTICAL

      So Release drops symbols and Debug keeps them. Reproducibility is what makes verifying a
      published checksum mean anything, and that only matters for the artifact users download; line
      numbers in a stack trace only matter while developing, where the build is right there.
    -->
    <DebugType Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">none</DebugType>
    <DebugType Condition="'$(Configuration)' != 'Release'">portable</DebugType>

    <!-- The NuGet cache path also leaks into some generated files; keeping restore deterministic
         means a rebuild resolves exactly the versions the lock/props pinned. -->
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' == 'true'">false</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
